<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tórshavn</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thorshavn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tórshavn is the capital and largest town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, on the east coast of the island of Streymoy. Its name means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a>'s harbour. The assembly of the islands met on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a> peninsula there from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, and the town later grew as the centre of the Danish trade monopoly. It is now the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logting">Løgting</a> and the Faroese government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torshavn.jpg" alt="Tórshavn" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PanWoyteczek · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Is03002-Torshavn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlers of the 9th century held their parliaments, called tings, at neutral and uninhabited places so that no location favoured anyone. The main ting met on the flat rocks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a>, the peninsula that divides the harbour in two, which was the most central place in the islands although no settlement stood there. The Færeyinga Saga says the ting of the Faroese was on Streymoy at the harbour called Tórshavn. A market grew beside the ting and gradually became a permanent trading area, and Tórshavn was never a distinct farming community like other Faroese villages.</p><p>In 1271 the Norwegian Crown set up a royal trade monopoly at Tórshavn, with two ships sailing regularly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> with salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and cereal. Officials of the Norwegian and then Danish administration made their home there. Around 1580 Magnus Heinason built the small fort of Skansin at the north end of the harbour, and in 1584 the town had 101 inhabitants. From 1655 to 1709 the von Gabel family held the islands and the monopoly, a period known as Gablatíðin, when islanders complained of unjust treatment and fixed prices. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> explosion burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a> in 1673, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in 1709 killed about 250 of some 300 inhabitants.</p><p>The town began to develop in the later 18th century, while Niels Ryberg ran the trade monopoly. From 1768 he carried on an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepot</a> trade, mostly smuggling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he was the first to think of profiting from fishing, with salted cod and herring. Tórshavn Cathedral was first built in 1788 and partly rebuilt in 1865.</p><p>On 30 March 1808, during the Anglo-Danish Gunboat War, HMS Clio entered the harbour and the fort at Skansin surrendered without firing a shot. The trade monopoly was abolished in 1856, opening the islands to free trade and putting Tórshavn at the centre of a changed economy. Its town council was founded in 1866, and in 1909 it became a market town with the same charter as Danish ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tórshavn is the capital and largest town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, on the east coast of Streymoy, with 14,223 inhabitants in 2024 and 22,444 in the greater urban area. Mountains rise to the northwest and southwest, separated by the Sandá River. It is the seat of the Faroese self-rule government, part of which still sits on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a>, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logting">Løgting</a>. Since 1990 it has been the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of the Faroe Islands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tórshavn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The islanders governed themselves through the Alting at Tinganes, in what is now <b>Tórshavn</b>, one of the oldest parliamentary sites in the world.</blockquote><a href="/state/faroese-commonwealth">Faroese Commonwealth</a></li></ul></section>
